Z||000020||FNXRISING||11-22-2000||12:56 AM||||Hello all!  Well, I'm new here, and I have a down payment on my my (soon-to-be) 88 yellow GT, but I DO have the privilege to own a Delorean as well as a Bricklin ( another gull-wing car).  <P>First off, let me put the "digital dash" problem to rest.  No, a digital dash was not a factory option on any of the 1981, 1982, or 1983 Deloreans.  BUT...  I have seen a Delorean with a digital dash.  Aftermarket.  Just as I have seen Fiero GTs with digital (aftermarket) dashes.  <P>As far as Deloreans go, I love them.  I have owned two over the last six years, and I simply think they are beautiful.  I think they are incredibly stylish, in a timeless fashion.  They look as "cool" or as "exotic" now as they did brand new.  And as for unique, how many gull wing doored cars have you seen?  Add to that, how many stainless steel cars have you seen?  The Delorean is BOTH of these!  And with only 8,500 or so made and with around 6,000 left on the road, you get TONS of attention!  <P>Do they have their quirks?  Sure they do.  Name ANY car that doesn't.  Are those quirks huge deals?  Not by any means.  There is a MASSIVE support system out there, just like for our Fieros.  Almost all parts are accessible at reasonable prices.  Most of the problems are minor, and have been "fixed" by several ingenious people out there.  <BR>I'm in the Northwest Delorean Club, and we have over 100 members.  The Delorean mailing list is extremely helpful, and there are at least four major parts warehouses in the US.<P>Powerhouse?  No.  Can be with some changes, but not stock-wise.  It, like the Fiero GT, also has a 2.8 V6, a motor called a PRV6. Basically a Volvo motor, with Peugeot heads, and Renault racing transmission.  Almost all electrical is Bosch.  Beautiful leather interior!  Very comfortable.  In fact, far more comfortable than most of the other cars I own or have owned.  I'm 6 foot 4, and I have lots of room.  John Delorean was the same height, and he helped design it to be comfortable for even a tall person.  I think its easy to get in and out of, and the door only requires less than a FOOT of space to fully open!  Try parking a foot away from another car or a wall in a Fiero, or any other car, and attempt getting in or out!  Power door locks, power windows, AC, interval wipers, four wheel disc brakes, dual wishbone suspension, more than decent storage space.  Easy to care for exterior-  no paint to worry about.  You can use a stainless steel cleaner and a Scothbrite pad and it looks gleaming!  Designed by Guirgiaro of Lotus. <P>Whew!  There's more, but that's probably more than most wanted to hear!  <P>If you have any questions, post them and I'll answer them for you. <P>Take care!<P>fnxrising||||reg||6
Z||000021||Cheever2||11-22-2000||05:04 AM||||That was very impressive.  Darn near got me sold on it.  Well not really cause I'm financially challenged.  But <B>after</B> the purchase, must one have money to burn to keep one's Delorean roadworthy?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||FNXRISING||11-22-2000||05:17 AM||||Not really.  The motor itself is extremely reliable.  Amd most of the Deloreans out there now have many of the modifications already, so the maintenence is normal.  My problem these days is getting somebody to properly rebuild my alternator!  I just had it done three months ago, but it has been dropping in power and has finally just died!<P>Most of the difficulty is in finding not just a good and honest mechanic, but one that isn't afraid of working on the D!  People think its a Ferrari or Lambo when you either mention it or drive up in it, and their eyes either turn into dollar signs, or they say they won't work on them.  Come on, these are just CARS for Pete's sake!<P>They really are fun.  I figure I will own one for the rest of my life.  I just can't see myself ever wanting to miss the attention/awe/misunderstanding/head-turning, etc.  Although I could do without the Back To the Future jokes...<P>fnxrising||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fiero5||11-22-2000||12:58 PM||||I have found that if you want something bad enough, you can live with whatever it throws at you afterwards. If you have always wanted a Delorean, the little pain in the butt stuff that comes up like on any car doesn't matter in the decision making process.<BR>Find a good buy, get it, and enjoy.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Tigger||11-22-2000||01:04 PM||||When I did some searching on the net a long time ago I thought I remember a few sites offering parts for the D, almost like the Fiero Store does for our cars.  Not sure if they charge a bunch of $$$ for D parts, but I thought it was pretty cool.  I remember even a site for a shop that does collision work on D's.||||reg||1
Z||000025||82-T/A||11-22-2000||01:05 PM||||I drove one a few times. I had this friend who owned one. The best thing about the car, is the novelty.. everyone instantly thinks Back to the Future when your cruising down the road and wonders where the Flux Capacitor is.<P>But as far as being a Performer.. it's extremely slow. I think it's safe to say it's at least a full second slower than a fully loaded bloated 88 GT.<BR>It also doesn't handle as well. <P>It is very similar to the Fiero, but as far as performance goes.. it's nothing compared to the Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||1
