Z||000032||DougV||11-23-2000||01:33 PM||||I'm a DeLorean and Fiero owner. Enjoy driving both! We have lot's of DeLorean owners in New Jersey that get together every month or so.<BR>Doug V      NJ<BR>83 DeLorean<BR>85 Fiero GT<BR>86 Fiero SE<BR>86 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000033||FNXRISING||11-23-2000||03:18 PM||||JohnnyK is right:  Bricklins are not even close to the Delorean.  But they came with two different motors.  The first year and a half they were powered by the AMC 360, until AMC had difficulty in promising supply.  At that point, a Ford 351W was used.  As for the transmission, I "think" a few manuals were made, but almost all were automatics.  The reason for this was safety.  The name of the car was the SV1 (Safety Vehicle One).  Everything on the car was supposedly made with safety in mind, all the way from the full roll cage down to no cigarette lighters.<P>But, the car just doesn't feel classy.  It does have raw power-  I can roast the tires from the get go.  But, it feels like a kit car.  It actually was a production vehicle.  They made around 2185, but there were some put together after the company folded, (jsut like the Delorean), and my VIN # is AFTER the last supposed VIN # produced!  Hmmm...<P>I'll post or email more info if desired later.  Its turkey-time for my family!<P>Take care!<P>fnxrising||||reg||1
Z||000034||JohnnyK||11-23-2000||03:53 PM||||How about a photo from the back of the Bricklin, D and Fiero lines up. I want to see how much lower the DeLorean is... a 45 inch high car is genius..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000035||Slammed Fiero||11-23-2000||04:38 PM||||Briklins were built here in Canada and were a huge FLOP , for good reason there lemons , if the body panels didn't fit right the electricals were screwed , the bodies were shipped in from Over seas , No where near the engineering of the Delorean, Who was the designer for the GTO when he worked at GM. The delorean is nice , but it's an orphan like the fiero , it's damm heavy and parts are expensive , Most of them had a volvo penta motor (I believe) and some had a renault motor. the car in back to the future that was seen in the action shots had a 355 small block chevy..<P>No digital dashes were ever offered from the factory int he cars short production run. NEVER!!! , I have a book on the history of John Delorean and the cars he influenced. Most of the cars came fully loaded. a few dumbasses out there have painted the cars , and there was one a while back in hemmings that was gold (CHEEZY) plated....<P><BR>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000036||JohnnyK||11-23-2000||06:06 PM||||No man, they all had PRV6's, and it was a stock DeLorean in back to the future. No digital dash was ever offered.. I'm not asking you guys, i'm telling you... Sheesh.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
