Z||000038||JBGehling||11-23-2000||10:29 PM||||Damn, too bad about the Bricklins... they look really cool in pictures  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">! Speaking of which, got any pictures of the delorean and bricklin on the computer?<P>Also, I'm going in the morning to look at an 81 Delorean (supposedly in perfect condition, with 19,000 miles...) Just wondering what a reasonable price for the car would be if it was in really great condition? NADA lists it as $23,175 high value (and the owner wants 24.5 OBO). <BR>Josh<P>Can I get any Delorean experts here to give me a link to a site that tells the difference between the 81, 82, and 83 models? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000039||Slammed Fiero||11-24-2000||04:36 PM||||No the back to the future car has a 355 , I have pics , My friend works at ILM & sky walker sound , they do a lot of movie stuff , owned by lucas film , they had the car for a while and were using it to develop a series of commercials for universal studios theme park. I have extensive photos of the car that is claimed to be the car used in all the action shots of Back to the Future. ||||reg||1
Z||000040||JohnnyK||11-24-2000||05:01 PM||||I'm 99.9% sure I read they just used a stock DeLorean, but if you say so.......||||reg||1
Z||000041||FNXRISING||11-25-2000||01:44 AM||||JBGehling...  <P>For that price the car had better be IMMACULATE.  Rarely does a Delorean command that high of a price.  There is a general rule when dealing with Deloreans:  There is a magic nimber of around $20,000.  Either you buy a crummy Delorean in fixer-upper shape for, say, $7,000 to $14,000 and spend the rest up to that $20,000 mark to get it in excellent shape, or you go out and find one in excellent shape and expect to pay...  yep: $20,000. <P>For the asking price of the one you are going (or already went) to see, it should be in concourse condition, or have so many cool mods or a motor exchange to warrant that extra cash.<P>Good luck!  Tell us what you think afterwards.<P>fnxrising||||reg||1
Z||000042||JBGehling||11-25-2000||11:28 AM||||I just saw the delorean yesterday morning and that is one sylish automobile! (this was the first delorean I'd ever looked at in person). The car seemed to have nothing outwardly wrong with it (the side of one of the seats had a minor rip, the digital clock didn't work and the engine ran barely above average). The good things about the car: it was turbo-charged, the back delorean words engraved into the bumper had been filled in with stainless steel (a damn awesome looking mod) and another exhaust pipe was added to the one that a turbo-charged car usually has. <BR>Josh<BR>About the owner: He had just donated 10 million dollars to USC (THE UNIVERSITY OF SOUTH CAROLINA). He had been out drinking with his friend, Johnny Carson when Johnny introduced him to John Delorean. He ended up buying a delorean and $75,000 dollars worth of stock within the next few weeks. <BR>This guy had enough delorean parts to start his own dealership (which he was going to give away with the car), he also had a delorean jumpsuit, two hats and much more stored away inside the trunk!||||reg||1
Z||000043||JBGehling||11-25-2000||11:30 AM||||BTW, he said he might go down a couple thousand (which still isn't enough for me, unfortunately)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. Doesn't look like I'm going to get this one.<BR>Josh||||reg||1
